Righto. I'll give you a couple to be going on with:

1) su and sudo are two different things. From your replies, you appear
not to be aware of this, or at the very least you confuse the two.

2) Just try it. Here's a recipe for sudo.

Log in as root, make a directory, `mkdir /test`. 
Check it's owner:group - should be root:root or similar.
Now login as a normal use and `sudo chown games:games /test`
Check the ownerships again. Magic! They've changed.
And now `sudo mkdir /test2 ; ls -l /test2 ; chmod root:root /test2 ;
ls -l /test2 ; rmdir /test2`. 

Well look at that. Everything has happened exactly as if you were
root. Because you _are_. Your real and effective UID and GID are set
to that of root.

3) For su, all you have to do is `su -`. After authenticating, you are
now root. Test away. su stands for "switch user", and with the - flag
is exactly as effective as logging in again.

I would invite you to admit to being wrong, but you fail to do that
utterly consistently every time everyone has had to correct your
fantasies.

